WEBVTT

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:06.780
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach University.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.780
Let us introduce testbenches.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.780
Testbenches are actually modules.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:18.780
These are modules that wrap around modules that we are going to test.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.780
We are going to refer to that one as Unit Other Test.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:29.780
Testbenches provide input, or more inputs if Unit Other Test has more than one input,

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:35.380
output, and also may check the values of the output for the module that we are testing.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:43.660
For the example that follows this tutorial, we are going to create a test bench according

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:49.580
to one of the examples, one of the videos that we provided with the tool support, and

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:56.100
our module is going to be called add underscore beh underscore tv. So this is the name of

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.380
the module that we are testing,

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:00.620
and we are adding underscore tv to it.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.620
And mentioned in that video,

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.900
the inputs and outputs do not exist.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.580
So basically more test bench doesn't have any ports.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.500
For each input of the original unit on the test module,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.580
we are going to create a register,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.920
and for each output, we are going to create wire.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.720
After that, we are going to instantiate the module.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.080
So we are going to create an instance

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.100
of a type add underscore beh.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.640
The name of that instance is going to be uut

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.080
and port connection is going to be as old here,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.900
more into one of the other videos.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.200
After that, we are going to place the body of the test bench

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.360
and end of the test bench is going to be

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.960
shown with end module keyword.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.240
So to visualize this concept,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.840
we can look at this picture.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:01.800
Entire portion that is marked in red

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.000
actually is TensorBench.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.280
So here in the body of TensorBench,

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.760
we are starting with initial begin and end input.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:15.760
And this is one walk that is going to provide inputs

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.400
inputs to the module on the test.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.480
And for each of the registers specified

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.900
in the signal declaration,

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:30.160
we are going to assign the value

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.800
and wait for designated amount of time.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.240
This value is going to be supplied

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:38.720
to our unit under test,

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.720
which is of type add underscore beh, add behavioral.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.800
It is going to, the module is going to produce

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.280
the values for out and for carry.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.240
These values can be observed using test bench

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.960
and checked against the correct values

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.680
or can be observed in waveform,

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.280
which is the method that you are going to use

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.190
in your lab one.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:16.110
So the full body of test bench from the beginning to end

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.670
looks as shown here.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.990
Your task will be to add additional cases

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.170
as per your lab specification.

